{"status": "ok", "message-type": "work", "message-version": "1.0.0", "message": {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 31]], "date-time": "2026-08-31T16:45:47Z", "timestamp": 1788194747779, "version": "build-2803163510"}, "reference-count": 35, "publisher": "SAGE Publications", "issue": "6", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2023, 7, 9]], "date-time": "2023-07-09T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1688860800000}, "content-version": "vor", "delay-in-days": 365, "URL": "http://www.sagepub.com/licence-information-for-chorus"}], "funder": [{"DOI": "10.13039/100000181", "name": "Air Force Office of Scientific Research", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "award": ["FA9550-15-1-0442, FA9550-18-1-0097"], "award-info": [{"award-number": ["FA9550-15-1-0442, FA9550-18-1-0097"]}], "id": [{"id": "10.13039/100000181", "id-type": "DOI", "asserted-by": "publisher"}]}, {"DOI": "10.13039/100000185", "name": "Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "award": ["Cooperative Agreement No.: HR00111820051"], "award-info": [{"award-number": ["Cooperative Agreement No.: HR00111820051"]}], "id": [{"id": "10.13039/100000185", "id-type": "DOI", "asserted-by": "publisher"}]}], "content-domain": {"domain": ["journals.sagepub.com"], "crossmark-restriction": true}, "short-container-title": ["The International Journal of Robotics Research"], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2023, 5]]}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Collision avoidance for multi-robot systems is a well-studied problem. Recently, control barrier functions (CBFs) have been proposed for synthesizing controllers that guarantee collision avoidance and goal stabilization for multiple robots. However, it has been noted that reactive control synthesis methods (such as CBFs) are prone to deadlock, an equilibrium of system dynamics that causes the robots to stall before reaching their goals. In this paper, we analyze the closed-loop dynamics of robots using CBFs, to characterize controller parameters, initial conditions, and goal locations that invariably lead the system to deadlock. Using tools from duality theory, we derive geometric properties of robot configurations of an N robot system once it is in deadlock and we justify them using the mechanics interpretation of KKT conditions. Our key deductions are that (1) system deadlock is characterized by a force equilibrium on robots and (2) deadlock occurs to ensure safety when safety is at the brink of being violated. These deductions allow us to interpret deadlock as a subset of the state space, and we show that this set is non-empty and located on the boundary of the safe set. By exploiting these properties, we analyze the number of admissible robot configurations in deadlock and develop a provably correct decentralized algorithm for deadlock resolution to safely deliver the robots to their goals. This algorithm is validated in simulations as well as experimentally on Khepera-IV robots For an interactive version of this paper, please visit https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.01781 .</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1177/02783649221074718", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2022, 7, 9]], "date-time": "2022-07-09T04:09:31Z", "timestamp": 1657339771000}, "page": "317-336", "update-policy": "https://doi.org/10.1177/sage-journals-update-policy", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 24, "title": ["The Before, During, and After of Multi-robot Deadlock"], "prefix": "10.1177", "volume": "42", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9021-0666", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Jaskaran", "family": "Grover", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "The Robotics Institute, Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, PA, USA"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Changliu", "family": "Liu", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "The Robotics Institute, Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, PA, USA"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Katia", "family": "Sycara", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "The Robotics Institute, Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, PA, USA"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "179", "published-online": {"date-parts": [[2022, 7, 9]]}, "reference": [{"key": "bibr1-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1007/s10514-017-9665-6"}, {"key": "bibr2-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1109/TAC.2016.2638961"}, {"key": "bibr3-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1016/j.ifacol.2015.11.154"}, {"key": "bibr4-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1017/CBO9780511804441"}, {"key": "bibr5-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1109/TRO.2004.839232"}, {"key": "bibr6-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1109/TRA.2004.824698"}, {"key": "bibr7-02783649221074718", "volume-title": "Multi-agent Collision Avoidance Using Interval Analysis and Symbolic Modelling with its Application to the Novel Polycopter", "author": "Douthwaite JA", "year": "2019"}, {"key": "bibr8-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1109/CONTROL.2018.8516848"}, {"key": "bibr9-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1142/S2301385019400065"}, {"key": "bibr10-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1109/ICSMC.2007.4414232"}, {"key": "bibr11-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1109/LRA.2019.2961302"}, {"key": "bibr12-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1177/027836499801700706"}, {"key": "bibr13-02783649221074718", "unstructured": "Grover J, Liu C, Sycara K (2019) Deadlock analysis and resolution in multi-robot systems (extended version). https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.09146."}, {"key": "bibr14-02783649221074718", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1016/j.ifacol.2020.12.2644"}, {"key": "bibr15-0278364